            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Passes positive values unchanged and multiplies each negative value by its own RANDOM slope, drawn evenly between lower and upper - a fresh draw for every element, every time a tensor arrives.\n\nThis is \"randomised leaky relu\" as torch uses it while a network is training: the random slope is a regulariser, noise that keeps a network from leaning on exact negative values. In a patch it is a leaky rectifier with a controlled amount of grain below zero: positive values come through clean, negative ones come out scaled down by a slightly different amount each time. The same input does NOT give the same output twice. For one fixed slope use t.nn.leaky_relu.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nOn the right, the input itself - a clean straight line. On the left, a ragged band rather than a line: each value is somewhere between the input times lower and the input times upper, and the band redraws on every frame. With the defaults (0.125 and 0.333) -4 comes out anywhere from -0.5 to -1.33. The band is narrow near 0 and widens towards the left, and lower equal to upper closes it into a single leaky relu line.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.rrelu\nNo arguments; defaults lower 0.125, upper 0.3333.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nlower / upper (drag floats on the node, and inlets):\nThe ends of the range the random slopes are drawn from. Defaults 0.125 and 0.3333. lower must not be above upper: the console reports \"Lower bound should be less than or equal to the upper bound\" and nothing is sent until they are back in order. Neither triggers the node: the next tensor uses them. In the demo the metro re-sends the ramp ten times a second, so the band redraws with new slopes each time and follows the sliders: drag lower and upper together to close the band into one line, apart to widen it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.leaky_relu uses one fixed slope, the same for every element. t.nn.relu is slope 0. t.rand and t.dist.uniform make the kind of random numbers the slopes are drawn from.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
